                <VentilationSystem>

Your house is generally air tight and does rely on the 5" fresh air supply pipe (connected to the return air plenum of the furnace) in order to provide "make up" air each time an exhaust fan (bathrooms &amp; clothes dryer) is operating. The house will become more air tight once the furnace is upgraded (eliminating the 6" chimney) and there are also a few pesky air leaks that you will want to address:



     - The perimeter of the attic hatch can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack



     - The wall switches &amp; receptacles on the exterior perimeter walls all have leakage and should have wall plate foam gaskets installed &amp; perhaps even upgrade the receptacles to the new "tamper proof" units that are safer for kids &amp; help reduce air leakage through the receptacle itself



     - The chimney for the current furnace, however this will be eliminated in conjunction with a furnace upgrade.



     - The main floor main bathroom fan was allowing alot of air leakage and it should either be inspected (to see if the damper is broken) or replaced with a new Energy Star rated unit.



If this house was being built today (including the renovation upgrades outlined in this report), it would have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed. In conjunction with your furnace upgrade, you should ask your contractor is they are licensed to design &amp; install HRV's - if so, they can offer further advice. They will likely look at 2 options:



1. Install an HRV that will utilize your new furnace to circulate the fresh air (and they will use the existing fresh air supply to the furnace as the fresh air supply to the new HRV)



2. Increase the "exhaust only" ventilation of your house by adding a timer or humidity sensor to the main floor main bathroom fan, and continue to use the 5" fresh air supply pipe as make-up air for the exhaust fans.



No matter which option you choose, please ensure the current wood stove has a dedicated outside combustion air pipe installed &amp; directly connected to the back of the wood stove.



If you decide to install an HRV, please consider installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) that is a minimum 70% efficient in transferring heat from the outgoing air to the incoming fresh air. The furnace can still be used to circulate the incoming "pre-heated" air throughout the house.



Savings:

By installing a 70% efficient ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, this will increase ventilation that will offset the savings from reducing the air leakage locations listed above, so it's approximately a "net no change". There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for installing an HRV. </VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. I have modeled replacing this hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 1,143 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 80% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled an upgrade from this older furnace to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing gas furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Note: Your current wood stove vents up a chimney &amp; does not have a dedicated outside combustion air pipe connected - so it draws its' combustion air from inside the house. As your house is quite air tight, this woodstove should have a dedicated outside combustion air pipe directly connected to it.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 1,945 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 46.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has three toilets and the one in the main floor rear bathroom is an older unit that uses a lot of water &amp; it should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the 3 x bathroom fans &amp; the clothes dryer - the kitchen range hood fan does not vent outside), indicated a pressure drop of 5.4 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace &amp; wood stove draw their combustion air from inside the house and are therefore susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood does not vent to the outside

        - There are three bathroom exhaust fans

        - There is a 5" fresh air supply connected to the "return air" ductwork of the furnace



The current furnace &amp; woodstove vent up separate chimneys &amp; there is no dedicated combustion air supply for either appliance. There is a 5" fresh air supply connected to the return air ductwork of the furnace that is meant to provide fresh air to the house &amp; "make-up" air for when other exhaust fans are operating (ie: bathroom fans &amp; the clothes dryer). This fresh air supply is not meant to be used as a combustion air supply, however I think (in your house) this is exactly what is happening !



In the "Heating System" section of this report, I provide recommendations to upgrade your furnace to a new high efficiency, direct vent model that will eliminate the need for the current furnace chimney. I also recommend you install a dedicated outside combustion air pipe that will be directly connected to your woodstove - so that it will burn outside air &amp;  not rely on combustion air from inside the house.



In the "Ventilation" section of this report, I highlight that your house is actually under-ventilated ...... including the air leakage that goes up the current furnace chimney &amp; with the 5" fresh air supply. As you complete the furnace upgrade (eliminating the current chimney), the house will become even tighter. If your house was being built today, it would have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed. If you choose not to install an HRV, you could increase the "exhaust only" ventilation (with additional make-up air from the 5" fresh air supply) by installing a timer or humidity sensor switch on the main floor bathroom fan.



You should consider replacing the bathroom exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR rated fan that use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels - and they have good internal dampers to reduce heat loss when they are not in use. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



